{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-94731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-1)^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1-9 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & =(-1)^{2}+3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+27 \\\\  & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94731" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{1}) & =1\\cdot 1=1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({x}_{2}) & =1\\cdot (-1)=-1<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94744" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the forms could be positive-definite"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{2}^{2}+7{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(-6)^{2}+7\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =36+0 \\\\  & =36>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -9 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-72<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={0}^{2}-(-6)^{2}-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-36-0 \\\\  & =-36<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94734" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is indefinite\n- $A$ is negative semi-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, by computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since both eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive semi-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=10$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive semi-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94795" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94806" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=-3$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and  III are false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, neither the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ nor the matrix $A$ is positive-definite or negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-139462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is positive-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa9e92a0659a96020f9a138750b45a5c38e3ada5992f76d80682366ccc2da50"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "q-94467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/state.json" "file_sha256" "55bd83f338e51028c1ddd76d066404df351762d05d6ba0a41141a537a0a6fcfb"} {"question" "e-8262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/cc0456884aa670c8c7f86387574a187f151f4ccc4e4275671425af1aa61812e8.png) By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/acf8b72ee7b927e5e82570c655a671a62b595a29c8191a64ad22ac98af8cf7a4.png)\n\n- $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8262.json" "file_sha256" "a61893f04d1c925d44d51b7edff639a854e3b9aea0e395e6a13d073a9fabbe8e"} {"question" "e-8262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/29545f79f3b34ca5395f98fa8c8c81e289482e866c3ad8c576aeb7b9fe8b8749.png)\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={2}^{2}-3\\cdot {7}^{2}+5\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =4-147+70 \\\\  & =-73<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =110>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={7}^{2}+8\\cdot 2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =49+112 \\\\  & =161>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8262.json" "file_sha256" "a61893f04d1c925d44d51b7edff639a854e3b9aea0e395e6a13d073a9fabbe8e"} {"question" "e-8263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d16c12fa9086e956b3b0516d42b40aaa121e3b40bd25a72c3d05d436ba410440.png) Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8263.json" "file_sha256" "2f77d8c52e1a480d41c29eea1cdfa6a319d7a01ad8722f01a5595e3d63b94ce7"} {"question" "e-8263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8263.json" "file_sha256" "2f77d8c52e1a480d41c29eea1cdfa6a319d7a01ad8722f01a5595e3d63b94ce7"} {"question" "e-8264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/assets/d81ce57017adec7daf2556322259f32ce4261e646dc8f1a48632beb085d9a9cb.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n\n- $A$ is positive-definite" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8264.json" "file_sha256" "176f5d80542ff74a552838f10c7b2b7ac18224fa213f430951b59d521f0aabc6"} {"question" "e-8264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i <          0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6.$ Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-5)|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-6)(-2)+(5)(-7)+12 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}|=37>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-11<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x},$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070500/example-8264.json" "file_sha256" "176f5d80542ff74a552838f10c7b2b7ac18224fa213f430951b59d521f0aabc6"}] "usage" {"q-94467" [] "q-94465" [] "q-94462" [] "e-8263" [] "e-8264" [] "e-8262" []} "basis" 2488 "retractions" [[17592186162560 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n1. $R(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{1}x_{2}$\n2. $S(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n3. $T(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2}$"] [17592186162563 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $λ_{1} = 7$ and $λ_{2} = 10$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is indefinite"] [17592186162565 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-1$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite"] [17592186161732 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n1. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$"] [17592186161732 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n$R(x)|= 2^{2} - 3 \\cdot 7^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 4 - 147 + 70; =-73 < 0$.\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$S(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = 110 > 0$.\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$T(x)|= 7^{2} + 8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 49 + 112; = 161 > 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186161733 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite"] [17592186161733 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwe have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A$, we obtain\n$\\mid A - λI \\mid |= 0;\\begin{vmatrix}-4 - λ & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix}|= 0; (-4 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 25|= 0; λ^{2} + 8λ - 9|= 0; (λ - 1)(λ + 9)|= 0; λ|= 1,-9$\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186161734 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite"] [17592186161734 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6$. Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-5)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)(-2) + (5)(-7) + 12; =-11$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} =-6 < 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid - 6\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 37 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-11 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$, and the matrix $A$, are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186160993 17592186160994 17592186160995 17592186160996 17592186160997 17592186161011 17592186161012 17592186161013 17592186161014 17592186161015 17592186161023 17592186161024 17592186161025 17592186161026 17592186161027] "immutable_fields" [[17592186160992 :answ